{
	title: 'Block headers as in the old Hal: time, who, model, effort',
	spec: `Prompts, model text and thinking are drawn as the old Hal drew them: a header row, one blank row, then the body. The header is the local time HH:MM of the block and who wrote it: 'You' for the user's prompt; 'Message from <label>' for one another session or a background job sent (the label task rj keeps, e.g. 'tab 3: Review rendering'); 'Hal (<model>)' for model text; 'Hal (<model>, thinking <effort>)' for thinking, or 'Hal (<model>, thinking)' when the effort is not known. Examples: '10:52 Hal (Opus 5.5)', '10:49 You', '10:51 Hal (Opus 5.5, thinking high)'. <model> is the model's display name from models.dev (task qq), else its id without the provider prefix. The header and body wear the block's colours (task 1w): a prompt's background paints one blank row above the header, the header, the blank row and the body, and one blank row below the body, as the old Hal padded bg cards; text and thinking have no background. The body is indented by one column. Tool calls, results, questions, commands, output and turn ends keep their one-row marker forms. The block id link of task wc sits at the right edge of the header row (a prompt's second row, below its blank top row); the header's title is clipped before it, never wrapped.

The header must be right from the block's first streamed byte and never change after (a header that appears or changes when the block is saved moves every row below it). So what it shows is recorded where the block is made: the assistant history record and the live event that starts a block carry the model id that produced it and, when known, the effort, and every shown item carries its ts. Effort is known when the request set it (a model chosen with a forced effort, OpenAI reasoning.effort, an Anthropic effort setting) or the API reported it; adaptive thinking with no effort set and no report shows 'thinking' alone. Old records without model or effort show 'Hal' and 'Hal (thinking)'; old records keep reading (tasks/README.md).

The title text (time, who, model, effort) comes from one function in src/common, so the web card head shows the same words as the terminal header. Reference: old Hal's transcriptTitles and blocks.blockLabel (artifact old-transcript-titles.ts); copy the words, not the structure. Tests: each kind of header for a prompt, a message from a tab, text and thinking with and without effort; a streaming block's header row is the same text on its first and last frame and after it is saved; a narrow terminal clips the title and keeps the id; replaying a history without model fields draws 'Hal'.`,
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['wc', 'qq'],
	foldInto: ['cc', 'wc', '4s'],
	notes: [
		"Supersedes wc's note that hal2 draws no header rows: the id moves from the leading marker token to the header row for prompts, text and thinking. Old header drawing: ~/.hal/src/client/terminal/blocks.ts (buildHeader, blockLabel, padBlock, renderBlock); canceled/queued/steering suffixes there are not wanted unless hal2 has the state.",
		"Where the facts live: history 'assistant' records carry model and effort and a ts that is the block's first streamed event (history.record's starts), not its save time; live 'stream' events carry that ts, turn-start carries model and effort, and snapshots' LiveTurn carries model, effort and per-block ts. So a client folding the live stream and one replaying history build equal items (turns.test 'a streaming block has its final header').",
		"Effort comes from provider.effort(model) (OpenAI's reasoning.effort today); Anthropic requests set none, so its thinking shows 'thinking' alone. titles.modelName moved here from status-row (qq).",
		"Web: a pending (unacknowledged) row shows no head, as it may turn out a command; prompt cards lose the '> ' mark and the '(sent from …)' line, both now in the head. Browser tests reading a card's textContent must skip .who.",
		"Startup budget (kn): headers add ~3 ms to the host's first draw, so titles.time caches per minute string; the joining-UI figure flakes past 100 ms at HEAD before hp too."
	]
}
